  {
    "episode_id": 92681,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92681.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "Well, we expect to vote on a report to hold them in criminal contempt this evening. It'll then go to the House, and if it passes theirs, we expect it will. It'll be referred to the Justice Department, which has a statutory duty to present it to the grand jury. And it will mean that he will be prosecuted. He will face up to a jail, a year in jail, or $100,000 fine or both. until he complies with the lawful demand for his testimony. So it will be, in my view, a powerful sign that our democracy is recovering, that no one is above the law. People can't simply not show up when there's subpoena to appear. So it's important in its own right because we need the information that he possesses. Why, for example, he predicted the day before the insurrection that all hell was gonna break loose on January 6th. But it's also important to send a message to other witnesses that they need to comply with law.",
