  },
  {
    "episode_id": 92681,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92681.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "Yes, I mean, the wheels are turning and turning very quickly. Banden didn't show up as recently as last week, and we are voting tonight. And I'm certain we'll have a vote very soon in the House. What people are familiar with in the past when witnesses fail to appear, like Don McGahn, the former White House Council under Donald Trump, We had to sue him in civil court and take it all the way up to the Supreme Court and all the way back down. It took two years before we ultimately got his testimony. But the reason we had the new civil litigation is that then attorney Bill Barr would not prosecute someone like McGahn for violating the lawful demand for his testimony. Bill Barr, after all, was held in concept himself. Bill Barr was involving himself in specific criminal cases where people had been indicted for perjury, for lying to Congress, and he was trying to reduce their sentences. And in the case of Michael Flynn trying to make their case go away completely. So enforcement through the criminal justice system wasn't possible when you had Bill Barr running interference for those covering up for Donald Trump. But now we have a different justice department and prosecution is a viable remedy. And we have an attorney general who believes in the rule of law and that no one should be above that law. Because I'll tell you, if any of my constituents were subpoenaed to appear before court proceeding or congressional proceeding and simply refused to, they would be arrested and Steve Bannon should be too.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_05",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
