    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92681.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 15,
    "turn_text": "It is not, I think, unexpected from someone who tried to use litigation during his presidency and abused litigation as a private party before he became president. And so, you know, he is a, you know, what you would call a vexatious litigant, someone who uses the court system for purposes of delay and sometimes for the purposes of just trying to inflict costs on his adversaries. So not unexpected, but it will fail. There is no, I think, meritorious claim of executive privilege that cannot be waived by the current president. And Joe Biden has said that he is not going to assert privilege here. That it's too important that the American people get the information that Congress needs to protect them. about all that went into January 6th and the former president's role and out of cabinet members and others, the role of the white nationalist groups in that rally and that attack, what the president knew or didn't know about the propensity for violence that day. So the current administration, which is the dominant party when it comes to any assertion of privilege, both for the current and previous administrations, has made it clear the public interest, clearly outweighs any considerations of privilege and that is, that's going to be given the ultimate weight by the courts.",
