    "turn_idx": 40,
    "turn_text": "or the other members of our committee. I had asked them to assemble with me and watch Mueller's testimony in the Judiciary Committee, because that testimony was going to immediately precede his testimony at our committee. And I wanted to make sure that we reacted to anything that was said or not said, And as we watched in the bunker, and I saw how much he had changed and how difficult even simple questions seem to be to answer, you know, I was shocked. And I told the members, you need to cut down the number of your questions. You need to cut down the length of your questions. If you think of questions too long, it is too long. If you're called for a narrative answer, you're not going to get a narrative answer. We need to page references of his report already and accessible. And it was hard to watch. It was hard to write about. I wanted to be respectful. I want to be respectful because I have such admiration for him. But there was no ignoring the impact of that halting testimony. We did hope, even though I wasn't expecting it to, as I mentioned, to lead to a march towards impeachment, we did hope that he could bring the report to life in a way that the document itself could not and would not. And that didn't happen. He was not able to do that.",
