    "turn_text": "Well, I can understand how astonishing you find this. I share the disbelief. On that day, on January 6th, I described what it was like to be on the house floor and to see the doors being battered and the windows breaking and to feel the fear of that day. but also the anger that came afterwards. And a lot of my anger was directed and is directed at what I describe as the insurrectionists and suits and ties. The people outside the building that were battering police and gouging them and whispering them, they believed the big lie. But the people in the chamber, they knew it was a big lie and they were really to push it even after the insurrection when we returned to the house floor and there was still blood on the ground where Ashley Babbit had been shot. Even then, they continue to push the big lie. Even today, they continue to push the big lie. I think this is really what places the country in the greatest parallel. If we can't trust that our elections can be used to decide our differences and who should represent us, it just leads to violence. It has led to violence. And they're still pushing it. You're absolutely right. And you know, one of the things that I, reasons I wrote this book is there's been a lot written about what took place inside the Trump White House and not very much written about what took place in the Congress",
