  },
  {
    "episode_id": 92681,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92681.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 56,
    "turn_text": "Well, the most often question I get from my constituents and others around the country is do the Republicans really believe what they're saying? What do they say when they're in private? And I share this anecdote because I want people to know what they say private. And it was about sitting next to Ken McCarthy on an airplane flying back to Washington, D.C., on United. This was long before Trump, but it shows how Ken McCarthy was really made for this moment when his party has no adherence to the truth. So we're sitting next to each other on the plane. having a complete nothing burger of a conversation. The kind you have when you're waiting for the movie to start, any movie to start. And we're talking about the 2010 midterms, which we're still six months away. I was saying I thought Democrats would win the midterms, and he was saying he thought Republicans would win the midterms. Nothing remarkable, unusual, or surprising in any way. And we get to Washington, and we go our separate ways. And unbeknownst to me, he goes off and does a briefing for the press. And he tells the press, Republicans are gonna win the midterms, everybody knows it. He sat next to Adam Schiff on the plane, and Adam Schiff admitted Republicans were gonna win the midterms. And I learned about this the next morning and I'm just beside myself. And I go rush up to him on the house floor and I said, Kevin, I would have thought if we were having a private conversation on the plane that we were having a private conversation. But if we weren't, you know, you tell the press the exact opposite of what I said. And he looks at me and he says, yeah, I know Adam, but you know how it goes. And you know, for all too many of them, that's how it goes. They will say whatever they need to say, want to say to gain power. In that respect, he was made for a moment when his party's leader had no adherence to the truth. I believe the truth wasn't truth, that he was entitled to his own alternate facts. Of all the things that the Trump administration and its enablers like Kevin McCarthy would do over the last four years among the most destructive was this relentless assault on the truth.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_05",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
