    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92681.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 69,
    "turn_text": "the case of Benghazi, there were probably five or six or seven investigations of Benghazi before the Select Committee on Benghazi was even formed. All of those prior investigations debunked the conspiracy theories of Benghazi. And I was involved with one of them in the Intelligence Committee. It was chaired by a Republican, Mike Rogers, and it completely debunked of these conspiracy theories. Kevin McCarthy, though, had something different in mind. He wasn't interested in getting to the truth or the facts of Benghazi was interested in taking down Hillary's polling numbers. And that was obvious from the very beginning of the Benghazi investigation. And in a way, and I read about this in the book, the Benghazi investigation was a harbinger of things to come. It was an illustration that Republicans were willing to create an alternate reality, even around the tragic death of four Americans, for the most political and partisan of reasons. That is the very different situation than investigating a violent attack on our Capitol, the first such investigation into all of what led up to that day and its aftermath. So a very different story with what we're trying to accomplish in getting to the truth. We're working in a bipartisan way to do it with two other Republicans who understand that the mission is to get to the truth. And we want to write a comprehensive report like the 9-11 Commission that will help protect the country in the future. So again, I guess this is in the same category we were discussing earlier. And that is how much priority do you put on moving forward and how much priority do you put on accountability so you can move forward. I think there needs to be accountability. The last point I would make is none of what we're doing in terms of investigating the insurrection is interfering, slowing down or inhibiting our ability to get an infrastructure bill and a bill back better bill passed, which I'm confident we will do. And the reason it hasn't been done already has nothing to do with our investigation in January 6, but the fact that an evenly divided Senate near unanimity, which is what we have in the Democratic Caucus, is not enough. We need unanimity and getting those extra handful of votes is essential, and we will do it. So I think you'll find, and I hope soon, that we're able to do both at the same time, because we have to.",
