    "conversation_move_label": "Answer"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 92681,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92681.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 74,
    "turn_text": "You know, it's a really important question. And after, particularly after the insurrection, a lot of my Democratic colleagues, you know, vowed that they were never going to work with anyone who had voted to de-certify the results. They were done with them. And I wrestled with what to do. But at the end of the day, I realized that on the committee that I chair the intelligence committee, more than, you know, more than half the Republicans on that committee voted to overturn the election. And if I refuse to work with them on anything else, none of the work of the committee would get done because the work of the intelligence committee is very difficult, very complicated. It's not partisan, but because it is often controversial, both sides need to come to agreement to put in place new privacy protections and make sure the agencies are talking to each other in the way that they should and make sure that agencies aren't talking to each other that are not permitted to. And so I have to work with my colleagues, and we have been able to get the work of the committee done, and I have to be able to compartmentalize, and yes, it is difficult. but the work needs to get done. At the same time, I write with candor about what my colleagues did and how much I think that they will be held accountable. As I said during the Senate impeachment trial, those that refuse to stand up to Donald Trump would have their names tied to his with the court of steel and for all of history. Because at the end of the day, it's the only thing that will ensure there's an incentive for people to do the right thing is knowing that if they don't, they'll be held accountable.",
