  {
    "episode_id": 92681,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92681.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 76,
    "turn_text": "Oh, I absolutely think he's running. I think it would be just, I think it would drive him mad if someone else got the attention as the Republican nominee. So I think Pathologic, he's not capable of not running. I think given the grip he has on his party today that he will be the nominee and that we have to take that candidacy very seriously. I also think that, you know, to the color, that as long as he's a leader of that party and as long as that party is not so much not at all really a party of ideas anymore or ideology but a cult an autocratic cult of the former president there's no accommodating their efforts to tear down our democracy they just have to be beaten at the polls but it's also sadly tragically The case that if you have a leader of one party, his whole motivation is to find new and inventive ways to divide Americans and push out vile every day. Just today, of course, he was attacking Colin Powell who just passed away and he couldn't even wait to to attack Colin Powell. Someone of that fundamental indecency is going to always contribute to a poisonous climate. And one of the things that so aggrieves me about what the Republican Party leadership did after the insurrection is you can see them toying with the idea of casting Trump overboard. You could see McConnell wrestling with whether he should try to do so. For McCarthy, those pangs of conscience lasted about 30 seconds. But for McConnell, you could really see the internal struggle. And yet he gave into that struggle. He decided that he could try to throw Trump overboard. He'd be thrown overboard himself. But at a certain point, you've got to ask, why are you there? If you're not going to defend the Constitution, why are you there? And so I would like to say that there's an easy path to restoring stability. In my own case, I try to not engage in the kind of name calling that you hear so often from the president to believe me, I'm tempted many times. But I don't think we're going to have the kind of return to the way it was until we have the supreme arsonist of our body politic off the scene for good.",
