  {
    "episode_id": 92681,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92681.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 81,
    "turn_text": "know, your point is exactly the right one. And one of my colleagues, Mike Quigley from Chicago, I'll put it better than anyone I've heard so far. When he said, it used to be that people would say, I'll believe it when I see it. Today, it's more people will only see it when they're ready to believe it. You can show people footage of the attack on the Capitol. You can show footage of people beating police officers. They'll still believe it was a normal tourist day. They won't see what's right in front of them because they're not ready to see it, because they still want to believe Donald Trump's lies. And frankly, there's still this huge amplifier in Fox News and Newsmax and OEM to give supporters of the former president a completely different world to live in. And you have the contribution in social media of algorithms that divide us and only show us what we want to see. It's really hard to break through that. And we recognize on this committee just what a difficult task that is. And part of meeting that challenge is holding hearings that will capture the public's attention like the hearing we did with those police officers so people can't look away. So they're forced to confront the truth. And we intend to have more of those hearings, and we intend to write a report that will hold up just as the 9-11 Commission report held up, definitive report about this tragedy, so that the history is preserved. And we will use that report to get out the message to the American people, to the best of our ability. But you're right, it's a tough job, made all the more difficult by something that Donald Trump had and has that I believe if Richard Nixon had had, if Richard Nixon had been the beneficiary of a Fox News channel devoted to becoming essentially his state-run TV and propagating his falsehoods, I think Richard Nixon would have never been forced to leave office. We're going to have to figure out how to be much better consumers of information. It's one of the cardinal challenges facing the country and facing the rest of the world.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_05",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
